WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.084
George Bush and Dick Cheney

00:00:01.084 --> 00:00:03.420
started new Wars in Iraq and Afghanistan.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.298
Obama started new Wars in Libya and Yemen.

00:00:07.298 --> 00:00:09.217
What new wars did Donald Trump start?

00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:10.719
You go down the list,

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:12.887
and when you look at things
like the destruction of Iraq

00:00:12.887 --> 00:00:14.848
or the implementation of a torture regime,

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:17.976
what has Donald Trump done
that even remotely compares

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:21.021
in terms of moral evil to any of that?

00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:21.688
Nothing,

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.815
and yet, no, we're supposed to treat

00:00:23.815 --> 00:00:25.191
George Bush and Barack Obama

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.777
like morally upstanding statesman

00:00:27.777 --> 00:00:30.739
and Donald Trump like the
literal reincarnation of Hitler.

00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:31.990
There's no journalist

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.242
who is more relentlessly iconoclastic

00:00:34.242 --> 00:00:38.038
than Glenn Greenwald who
won the 2014 Pulitzer Prize

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:40.915
for his reporting on
the Snowden revelations.

00:00:40.915 --> 00:00:43.043
Though unapologetically progressive,

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:44.961
the 53 year old former lawyer

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:47.297
never shrinks from fighting with the left.

00:00:47.297 --> 00:00:50.425
A week before the 2020
election he quit the Intercept,

00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:53.470
the online news site
he co-founded in 2014,

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:54.804
because by his account,

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:57.557
it refused to run a
story unless he removed

00:00:57.557 --> 00:01:00.143
all sections critical of Joe Biden.

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:02.604
Denouncing what he
called "the pathologies,

00:01:02.604 --> 00:01:05.148
illiberalism, and repressive mentality"

00:01:05.148 --> 00:01:07.692
to led him being censored
by his own media outlet,

00:01:07.692 --> 00:01:10.570
Greenwald railed that
"these are the viruses

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.363
that have contaminated virtually

00:01:12.363 --> 00:01:15.325
every mainstream center-left
political organization,

00:01:15.325 --> 00:01:17.660
academic institution, and news room."

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.996
Like a growing number
of refugees from more

00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:21.706
traditional news organizations,

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:23.792
Greenwald took his talents to Substack,

00:01:23.792 --> 00:01:26.377
a platform for independent
content creators

00:01:26.377 --> 00:01:29.380
to earn revenue directly
from their audiences.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.508
He wasted no time lobbing grenades,

00:01:31.508 --> 00:01:34.010
posting stories and videos with titles,

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.179
like No Matter the Liberal Metric Chosen,

00:01:36.179 --> 00:01:39.641
the Bush/Cheney Administration
was Far Worse than Trump.

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:42.602
And The Three Greatest
Dangers of Biden/Harris:

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.146
Militarism, Corporatism, and Censorship,

00:01:45.146 --> 00:01:47.023
All Fueled by Indifference.

00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:49.984
I spoke with Greenwald via
Zoom at his house in Brazil,

00:01:49.984 --> 00:01:51.319
where he lives with his husband,

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:53.404
two children, and numerous dogs.

00:01:53.404 --> 00:01:54.614
Among other topics,

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:57.325
we discussed what he sees
as a generational fight

00:01:57.325 --> 00:01:58.743
playing at a newsrooms,

00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:02.247
the challenge identity politics
poses to free expression,

00:02:02.247 --> 00:02:05.250
and whether a coalition of
libertarians and progressives

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.670
can effectively push
non-interventionist foreign policy,

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.965
lifestyle liberation, and an
end to corporate subsidies

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:14.175
during the presidency of Joe Biden.

00:02:16.427 --> 00:02:18.847
Glenn Greenwald, thanks
for talking to Reason.

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:22.851
Always happy to be with Reason.

00:02:22.851 --> 00:02:23.685
Good to see you, Nick.

00:02:23.685 --> 00:02:26.229
Yes, let's see.

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:29.399
Let's start with your
leaving the Intercept,

00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:32.944
which you announced less than
a month ago as we're talking.

00:02:32.944 --> 00:02:35.572
And in the piece that you wrote

00:02:35.572 --> 00:02:39.784
explaining why you left this amazing

00:02:39.784 --> 00:02:41.744
publication platform

00:02:41.744 --> 00:02:44.539
that you helped start
only a few years ago,

00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:46.124
you said that you were witnessing

00:02:46.124 --> 00:02:49.085
or experiencing "The same
trends of repression,

00:02:49.085 --> 00:02:52.338
censorship, and ideological homogeneity

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:55.008
plaguing the national press."

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.634
So what happened?

00:02:56.634 --> 00:02:58.595
Well, some of you may recall

00:02:58.595 --> 00:03:01.890
that when I created the Intercept

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.018
with Laura Poitras and Jeremy Scahill,

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:08.271
it was at the height of the
Snowden story back in 2013,

00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:10.440
I was at the Guardian at the time,

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.485
and I had received a lot
of support institutionally

00:03:14.485 --> 00:03:15.945
and editorially from the Guardian.

00:03:15.945 --> 00:03:18.323
But I began noticing as I worked

00:03:18.323 --> 00:03:20.158
with other media outlets
to report that story,

00:03:20.158 --> 00:03:22.035
a lot of internal obstacles

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:25.663
that I thought were quite
difficult to overcome

00:03:25.663 --> 00:03:27.749
in terms of doing the kind of reporting,

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:30.043
not just with that story, but in general,

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.170
I thought needed to be done.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:35.048
And because Laura and I
had a lot of visibility

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:37.967
with that story, and Jeremy had done

00:03:37.967 --> 00:03:42.972
a lot of high visibility
reporting of his own,

00:03:43.264 --> 00:03:44.724
including having produced a film

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:47.477
about Obama's war on
terror called "Dirty Wars"

00:03:47.477 --> 00:03:49.979
that had received an Oscar nomination,

00:03:49.979 --> 00:03:52.440
We had a lot of leverage to
create a new media outlet.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.567
And we obviously didn't do that

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:58.112
given that we all had very
good platforms at the time

00:03:58.112 --> 00:04:00.114
to replicate what was already being done.

00:04:00.114 --> 00:04:02.075
We only left the places we were at,

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:02.909
which were very secure,

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:04.452
because we thought we could do

00:04:04.452 --> 00:04:06.829
something different in journalism.

00:04:06.829 --> 00:04:09.499
And one of the principle visions

00:04:09.499 --> 00:04:12.627
we had was that

00:04:12.627 --> 00:04:14.837
we thought the model for how journalism

00:04:14.837 --> 00:04:17.257
is often conducted inside
corporate media outlets,

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:20.260
which is this hierarchal
top-down structure

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:23.554
where editors impose, not
necessarily an ideology

00:04:23.554 --> 00:04:25.723
as much as a kind of tone,

00:04:25.723 --> 00:04:27.809
so they flatten out the vibrancy

00:04:27.809 --> 00:04:31.104
and personality and voice in journalism,

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:32.480
make everything sound the same,

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.607
make everything adhere
to these very rigid rules

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:36.567
of how you can express yourself,

00:04:36.567 --> 00:04:38.403
what kind of views can be heard,

00:04:38.403 --> 00:04:39.737
with stifling journalism

00:04:39.737 --> 00:04:41.572
it was making it not just an effective,

00:04:41.572 --> 00:04:42.699
but actually quite boring.

00:04:42.699 --> 00:04:45.285
And the idea was it's
gonna be a journalism

00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:47.287
led media outlet where editors are there

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:50.248
to help you when you need
it to kind of kick the tires

00:04:50.248 --> 00:04:53.334
on stories to make sure that
things are factually sound,

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:54.460
but they're not the bosses.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.129
They're not the people
you have to overcome

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:58.339
who decide whether you
can be heard or not.

00:04:59.841 --> 00:05:01.342
And I had written into my contract,

00:05:01.342 --> 00:05:03.720
just like I did at the Guardian and Salon,

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.430
that except in very rare cases

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:08.099
where there was a very
complex original reporting,

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:09.100
like in the Snowden story,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.311
of the Brazil reporting we did last year

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:13.271
that I would just publish
directly to the internet

00:05:13.271 --> 00:05:15.773
with no editorial intervention.

00:05:15.773 --> 00:05:18.151
And that was the model we
thought we were building

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:19.277
that I thought I was building.

00:05:19.277 --> 00:05:20.445
I never thought it had anything

00:05:20.445 --> 00:05:24.282
to do with ideological dogma,

00:05:24.282 --> 00:05:27.910
and certainly never fealty
to any political party.

